From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PatchForge
Date: Sat, 6 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix top-level file lookup in RepoGraph structure walk

create_structure nests top-level files under the repo-basename key,
so structure == {basename: {'setup.py': ..., <subdirs...>}}. But
get_tags_raw walks rel_fname.split('/') from the structure root, which
only works for nested paths (first part == basename by coincidence).
Top-level files (rel 'setup.py') raise KeyError. Observed on
psf/requests @ a0df2cb: KeyError: 'setup.py'. OS-independent
upstream bug (would crash on Linux too for repos with top-level .py).

Fix: on KeyError, retry the walk from structure[basename(root)],
which is where top-level files live (same basename expression
create_structure itself uses, so consistent by construction).
Nested paths take the original fast path unchanged.

Apply from the RepoGraph clone root:
  git apply <workspace>/patches/repograph-toplevel-lookup.patch

Revert:
  git -C third_party/RepoGraph checkout -- repograph/construct_graph.py
  (then re-apply the other repograph-*.patch files if still needed)

Upstream status: not fixed upstream as of 2026-09-06.
Do NOT commit into third_party/; this file is the record (§24).

--- a/repograph/construct_graph.py
+++ b/repograph/construct_graph.py
@@ -218,9 +218,18 @@
 
     def get_tags_raw(self, fname, rel_fname):
         ref_fname_lst = rel_fname.split('/')
         s = deepcopy(self.structure)
-        for fname_part in ref_fname_lst:
-            s = s[fname_part]
+        try:
+            for fname_part in ref_fname_lst:
+                s = s[fname_part]
+        except KeyError:
+            # Top-level files live under the repo-basename key, e.g.
+            # structure == {'requests': {'setup.py': ...}} while
+            # rel_fname == 'setup.py'. Retry from that level.
+            s = deepcopy(self.structure)
+            s = s[os.path.basename(self.root)]
+            for fname_part in ref_fname_lst:
+                s = s[fname_part]
         structure_classes = {item['name']: item for item in s['classes']}
         structure_functions = {item['name']: item for item in s['functions']}
         structure_class_methods = dict()
